<!-- core:start -->
An ODF document (`.odg`, `.odp`, `.ods`) is a zip whose `meta.xml` is the
document's header. A drawing has no comment syntax, so `office:meta` is where the
provenance, licence and AI-contribution information lives: it is the equivalent
of the `<meta:header>` block and the `AIMetrics` block for a source file.

Every ODF file an agent creates or modifies must carry all of the fields below.
They are written by the script that generates the document, never by hand in a
GUI — see [Generating, not hand-editing](#generating-not-hand-editing).

## Required fields

| Purpose | Element | Value |
|---|---|---|
| title | `<dc:title>` | the human title, no trailing full stop |
| subject | `<dc:subject>` | one sentence: what the document is and who it is for |
| keywords | `<meta:keyword>`, one element per keyword | 3 to 8 terms; LibreOffice joins them with `", "` in the PDF |
| contributor | `<meta:user-defined meta:name="contributor" meta:value-type="string">` | the AI agent **and** the human, in the project's format (see the local block) |
| publisher | `<meta:user-defined meta:name="publisher" meta:value-type="string">` | the publishing organisation, from the local block |
| rights | `<meta:user-defined meta:name="rights" meta:value-type="string">` | the licence of the rendered document, from the local block |
| comments | `<dc:description>` | the full `AIMetrics` block, see below |
| byline | `<meta:initial-creator>` | the only element LibreOffice exports as the PDF `Author` |
| last editor | `<dc:creator>` | the human who approved the change |
| created | `<meta:creation-date>` | ISO 8601 (`YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss`), when the file was first generated |
| modified | `<dc:date>` | ISO 8601, last write |

## Why exactly these elements

Where the fields are visible matters when someone checks the document: in
LibreOffice, `title`, `subject`, `keywords` and the `comments` appear on
**File ▸ Properties ▸ Description**, while `contributor`, `publisher` and
`rights` appear on **File ▸ Properties ▸ Custom Properties**. There is no
Contributor, Publisher or Rights field on the Description tab, so a document
that looks incomplete there is usually correct.

Because that is invisible without a GUI, verify from the model instead:

```bash
python3 - <<'PY'
# run against an instance started with:
#   soffice --headless --accept='socket,host=127.0.0.1,port=2002;urp;'
import uno
from com.sun.star.beans import PropertyValue
ctx = uno.getComponentContext().ServiceManager.createInstanceWithContext(
    "com.sun.star.bridge.UnoUrlResolver", uno.getComponentContext()).resolve(
    "uno:socket,host=127.0.0.1,port=2002;urp;StarOffice.ComponentContext")
desktop = ctx.ServiceManager.createInstanceWithContext("com.sun.star.frame.Desktop", ctx)
doc = desktop.loadComponentFromURL(uno.systemPathToFileUrl("deck.odg"), '_blank', 0,
                                  (PropertyValue('Hidden', 0, True, 0),))
udp = doc.getDocumentProperties().UserDefinedProperties
ps = uno.invoke(udp, "queryInterface", (uno.getTypeByName("com.sun.star.beans.XPropertySet"),))
for p in ps.getPropertySetInfo().getProperties():
    print(p.Name, '=', ps.getPropertyValue(p.Name))
doc.close(True)
PY
```

ODF 1.3 `office:meta` has no `dc:contributor`, no `dc:rights` and no
`dc:publisher`. Writing any of those makes the file **invalid**, and the schema
says so:

```
$ xmllint --noout --relaxng OpenDocument-v1.3-schema.rng meta.xml
meta.xml:13: element rights: Relax-NG validity error : Did not expect element rights there
```

So publisher, rights and contributor are carried as `meta:user-defined`
properties, which the schema does allow and which LibreOffice preserves across a
save. The rest of the table is standard ODF.

`meta:initial-creator` deserves its own warning. It is the **only** source of the
PDF `Author`:

- `initial-creator` set, `dc:creator` set: the PDF `Author` is `initial-creator`.
- `initial-creator` absent, `dc:creator` set: the PDF has **no `Author` at all**.

Put the byline in `initial-creator`, and do not expect `dc:creator` to reach the
PDF. Everything in the "contributor", "publisher", "rights" and "comments" rows
is likewise ODG-only; see [`pdf-metadata.mdc`](pdf-metadata.mdc).

`meta:generator` is tool-owned: LibreOffice rewrites it on every save, so it must
not be used to record authorship. Record the tool chain in `contributor` and the
`AIMetrics` block instead.

## The AIMetrics block

`dc:description` carries the same JSON array that a commit message carries, with
one entry per **change** appended to the existing entries and none removed. The
`timestamp`/`interval` choice, the percentage and the `name`/`version`/`model`
rules are those of [`ai-metrics.mdc`](ai-metrics.mdc).

`dc:description` is plain text, so there is no comment syntax and no fence around
it: store `AIMetrics: [ ... ]` exactly as it would appear at the end of a commit
message.

## Verification

```bash
# 1. Every required field is present, with the right element names.
unzip -p deck.odg meta.xml | xmllint --format -

# 2. The metadata is schema-valid. Fetch the schema once:
curl -sLO https://docs.oasis-open.org/office/OpenDocument/v1.3/os/schemas/OpenDocument-v1.3-schema.rng
unzip -p deck.odg meta.xml > /tmp/meta.xml
xmllint --noout --relaxng OpenDocument-v1.3-schema.rng /tmp/meta.xml
```

Validate `meta.xml` only. Do **not** gate on `content.xml` or `styles.xml`:
LibreOffice writes `loext:*` extension attributes that the strict ODF 1.3 schema
rejects, and it does so in its own files, so a schema failure there is expected
and is not a defect in the document. Well-formedness is the test for those parts:

```bash
for part in content.xml styles.xml; do unzip -p deck.odg "$part" | xmllint --noout -; done
```

## Generating, not hand-editing

The metadata must come from the script that builds the document, for three
reasons: the `AIMetrics` history has to accumulate rather than be retyped; the
metadata has to be reproducible when the document is rebuilt; and a GUI save
rewrites `meta:generator`, the dates and the statistics, so a hand edit cannot be
reproduced from anything tracked.

A generated ODF artifact therefore belongs next to the script that produces it,
and that script is tracked. Rebuilding an artifact by hand in the GUI breaks this
rule even when the visual result is identical.

## Further points

- Append to the `AIMetrics` array; never replace existing entries, and never
  delete an earlier contributor.
- Keep the title and subject stable across rebuilds: they are what a reader sees
  in a file listing and in the PDF, and changing them for cosmetic reasons
  churns the artifact.
- Do not put secrets, tokens or internal URLs in any metadata field. All of them
  are exported into the PDF or visible in a zip listing of the document.
